S.append(("h1", "Abstract"))
S.append(("p", "Aromatase inhibitors (AIs) are endocrine-active pharmaceuticals that enter wastewater and also cause bone loss in treated patients, so one target links an environmental and a clinical outcome. Using network toxicology, we combined pharmacovigilance with multi-omics dissection of AI-associated osteoporosis. FAERS mining (2013-2026) showed consistent disproportionality signals for letrozole, anastrozole and exemestane (osteoporosis RORs 2.77-4.08), surviving stricter thresholds (ROR > 1.5; PRR > 2), with E-values of 4.98-9.32; 390 of 394 sex-annotated letrozole-osteoporosis reports concerned women. Intersecting 42 drug targets with 3,713 osteoporosis genes yielded nine core genes centred on CYP19A1. Two cohorts (GSE35958 MSCs, n = 9; GSE13850 B cells, n = 20) showed no core-gene differential expression, consistent with an exposure-driven signal. WGCNA and LASSO nominated VPS35 as a candidate network core. Blind docking placed both AIs in the CYP19A1 site and on the VPS35 surface, and docked at least as favourably into zebrafish Cyp19a1a (9/10 identical active-site residues); virtual knockout of VPS35 in bone-marrow single cells perturbed translation and cell-fusion programmes. A fish plasma-model scenario places the exposure-activity ratio for exemestane at 0.7 at 100 ng/L, approaching therapeutic plasma levels. These lines of evidence point to a testable AI-CYP19A1-oestrogen-VPS35 axis for bone monitoring and environmental risk assessment; all computational claims require experimental validation."))

S.append(("h1", "1. Introduction"))
S.append(("p", "Breast cancer is the most commonly diagnosed malignancy worldwide {{sung2021}}, and aromatase inhibitors (letrozole, anastrozole, exemestane) constitute the backbone of endocrine therapy for hormone-receptor-positive, HER2-negative disease {{burstein2021}}, and treatment algorithms continue to evolve {{burstein2022}}. By inhibiting aromatase (CYP19A1), these drugs suppress peripheral oestrogen synthesis {{cyp19_2027}} and remove the principal hormonal protection of bone. AI users experience rapid bone loss and fragility fractures {{rachner2011, hadji2025}}, a toxicity recognized since the early adjuvant trials and now managed through formal clinical-practice guidance {{coleman2020}}. The molecular pathogenesis of postmenopausal osteoporosis is increasingly well mapped {{osteopath2026}}, and bone-formation niche dysfunction is being dissected at single-cell resolution {{boneniche2026}}; bone-protective interventions during AI therapy are an active field {{ai_bone2025, romosozumab2026}}."))
S.append(("p", "Pharmacovigilance databases provide a complementary, real-world layer: disproportionality analysis of spontaneous reports has matured into a standard signal-detection methodology {{bate2009, vanpuijenbroek2002, openfda2016}}, and FAERS-based analyses of hormonal-therapy bone adverse events are accumulating {{ai_bone_faers2026}}. Network pharmacology and its toxicology-oriented extension provide a framework for mapping compound–target–pathway–disease interactions {{hopkins2008, nogales2022}}. Most AI-bone analyses are either purely pharmacoepidemiological or purely molecular, and the two strands rarely meet. Bone-protective strategies are already recommended for AI users {{hadji2025, coleman2020}}, yet the molecular basis for stratification — who loses bone fastest, and through which programmes — remains unclear."))
S.append(("p", "Aromatase inhibitors are also environmental contaminants. They are excreted into municipal wastewater, and letrozole and tamoxifen have been measured in sewage sludge, letrozole mostly below quantification limits {{ai_env_sludge}}; the CYP19A1 orthologue they inhibit is conserved across vertebrates. In fish, trace letrozole exposure reprograms gonadal transcription in sex-undifferentiated Nile tilapia {{ees_letrozole_tilapia}} and disrupts the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis in zebrafish {{letrozole_zebrafish}}, while the intrinsic sensitivity of CYP19 to inhibition differs several-fold among freshwater species {{aromatase_fish_sensitivity}}. The same molecular target therefore couples an environmental endocrine-disruption question to a clinical bone-safety question, and a network-toxicology dissection of the CYP19A1 axis addresses both."))

S.append(("p", "Here we integrate five complementary layers: FAERS pharmacovigilance, target-disease intersection, transcriptomic re-analysis with WGCNA/LASSO, molecular docking and virtual knockout. We frame VPS35 as a falsifiable candidate axis (Fig. 1A). Results are reported for all cohorts, including those in which the core genes did not differ."))
S.append(("h1", "2. Materials and methods"))
S.append(("h2", "2.1 FAERS pharmacovigilance"))
S.append(("p", "Individual case safety reports (2013-2026) were retrieved through the openFDA drug/event endpoint (FAERS quarterly data) {{openfda2016}}. Reporting odds ratios (ROR) and proportional reporting ratios (PRR) were computed for letrozole, anastrozole and exemestane against 'osteoporosis' and 'osteoporotic fracture' (MedDRA preferred terms). Tamoxifen served as a positive sensitivity control because it is itself associated with skeletal effects. Age and sex stratification used the letrozole-osteoporosis reports before deduplication (253 with age, 394 with sex). The 2x2 tables used deduplicated counts: 405 reports reduced to 290 after removing 115 duplicates by case identifier, retaining the latest version of each case. E-values were computed as E = RR + √(RR(RR−1)) with the ROR used as an approximation of RR. The ROR was computed from the 2x2 table as (a/c)/(b/d) and the PRR as (a/(a+b))/(c/(c+d)). Here a denotes AI-event co-reports, b AI reports without the event, c event reports without the AI and d reports with neither; 95% intervals used the Woolf formula with a 0.5 continuity correction. Six primary tests (three drugs by two events) were run without correction, and sparse cells were interpreted as uninformative. Sensitivity thresholds (ROR > 1.5; PRR > 2) and age/sex stratification were fixed in the internal analysis plan before analysis {{bate2009}}."))
S.append(("h2", "2.2 Target and disease gene sets"))
S.append(("p", "AI targets were retrieved from ChEMBL {{chembl2024}} and SwissTargetPrediction {{swiss2019}}; osteoporosis-associated genes from GeneCards {{genecards2016}}. Drug targets and disease genes were intersected, and the resulting genes were mapped onto a STRING {{string2023}} protein-protein interaction network (combined score ≥ 0.4). GO enrichment used clusterProfiler {{cp2021}} with the whole expression matrix as background; the target and gene sets are listed in Table S1."))
S.append(("h2", "2.3 Transcriptomic re-analysis, WGCNA and LASSO"))
S.append(("p", "GSE35958 (low-BMD versus high-BMD donor bone-marrow MSCs; n = 9 samples; platform metadata as deposited in GEO {{geo2002}}) was re-analysed with limma {{limma2015}} on log2(x+1) values. Genes were called differentially expressed at BH-adjusted p < 0.05 with |log2FC| > 2 (GSE35958) or > 1.5 (GSE13850). The two monocyte cohorts were called on adjusted p alone, because their effect sizes were uniformly small (maximum |log2FC| 0.68). WGCNA {{wgcna2008}} was performed on the larger monocyte cohort GSE56815 (n = 80; signed network, soft-thresholding power from scale-free fit, minimum module size 30, modules merged below 0.25 height) instead of the nine-sample MSC cohort. Modules associated with BMD (BH-adjusted p < 0.05) defined the candidate pool as their top-50 kME genes together with the nine core genes. A logistic LASSO {{glmnet2010}} (binary outcome low versus high BMD, 10-fold cross-validation along the pre-specified lambda path) then selected the final candidate. We also re-analysed GSE13850 (circulating B lymphocytes; 10 high- versus 10 low-BMD of the 40-sample series) with the same pipeline. None of the nine core genes was differentially expressed in GSE13850 either, and the cross-cohort core-gene fold changes are summarised alongside the GSE35958 results. As a single-arm reference, core-gene and VPS35 FPKM were taken from GSE201774 (n = 16 osteoporotic donor MSCs)."))
S.append(("h2", "2.4 Molecular docking and virtual knockout"))
S.append(("p", "Letrozole and anastrozole were docked blind over the whole of aromatase (PDB 3S7S {{pdb2002}}) and human VPS35 with AutoDock Vina 1.2 {{vina2021}} (seed = 42, exhaustiveness 32, five poses retained). Poses were then rescored without optimisation using the Vinardo function on identical grids. The zebrafish orthologue Cyp19a1a (UniProt O42145) was modelled with AlphaFold2 (AF-O42145-F1, version 6; mean pLDDT 88.4) and docked with the same protocol to test cross-species transferability. Human VPS35 was represented by the AlphaFold2 {{jumper2021}} monomer model AF-Q96QK1-F1 (UniProt Q96QK1, model version 6; mean pLDDT 91.25) {{af2db2024}}. Blind docking scores without molecular-dynamics refinement are exploratory, and the near-identical CYP19A1 scores of the two AIs do not reproduce their experimental activity ranking, which limits confidence in the VPS35 predictions. Virtual knockout of VPS35 in pooled bone-marrow single cells (GSE343017, from AML patients and healthy donors) used scTenifoldKnk {{stfk2022}}. Analyses used R 4.6.1 {{rcore}} with seed = 42 and two-sided tests."))

S.append(("h1", "3. Results"))
S.append(("h2", "3.1 Pharmacovigilance signals"))
S.append(("p", "All three AIs showed osteoporosis signals: letrozole (ROR 2.77/PRR 2.76), anastrozole (4.08/4.05) and exemestane (3.61/3.59; 123 co-reports), with fracture signals for letrozole (4.93/4.92) and anastrozole (2.86/2.86); exemestane-fracture was uninformative (one co-report). All signals passed the ROR > 1.5 and PRR > 2 thresholds (Fig. 1B-D; Table S2). E-values ranged from 4.98 to 9.32, indicating that substantial unmeasured confounding would be needed to explain them. Letrozole-osteoporosis rested on 290 co-reports among 55,641 letrozole reports and anastrozole-osteoporosis on 204 among 26,603; fracture co-reports were rarer (25 and 7; full 2x2 tables in Table S2). Reports accumulated from 2013 (Fig. 1C). Among reports with age data (253/405) the modal group was < 65 years (n = 130), and 390/394 sex-annotated reports were female (Fig. 1E). Tamoxifen also gave a positive signal (ROR 2.94), consistent with monitoring or channeling shared by endocrine therapies; it is treated here as a positive control. The crossed drug-event ranking (letrozole highest for fracture, anastrozole highest for osteoporosis) may reflect prescribing patterns or channeling rather than intrinsic differences, and the exemestane osteoporosis signal points to a class effect (Fig. 1F)."))
S.append(("h2", "3.2 Target network and functional annotation"))
S.append(("p", "To connect these signals to molecular targets, we intersected the 42 drug targets with 3,713 osteoporosis genes; nine core genes were identified (CYP19A1, HDAC1, HDAC6, ACHE, ADRB1-3, HTR2A/2C; Fig. 2A-B). The STRING network (score ≥ 0.4) linked eight of the nine genes into seven edges, with CYP19A1 isolated and the ADRB1–3/HTR2A–2C cluster the densest component (Fig. 2C). The compound-target-pathway network is shown in Fig. 2D, the core-gene evidence matrix in Fig. 2E, GO enrichment in Fig. 2F and PPI edge confidence in Fig. 2G; panel-level detail is in Fig. S1-S6. Hormonal and adrenergic terms dominated the GO output: temperature homeostasis (5/9 genes; adjusted p = 6.6×10-6), adenylate cyclase-activating adrenergic receptor signalling (3/9; adjusted p = 1.3×10-5) and blood-pressure regulation (3/9; adjusted p = 1.3×10-5; Fig. 2F). This profile reflects the three ADRB paralogs and the serotonergic receptors. Across the three categories, 256 BP, 15 CC and 33 MF terms were enriched (adjusted p < 0.05; Table S3). Because three of the nine genes are ADRB paralogs, this enrichment partly reflects gene-set composition. ChEMBL targets alone yielded one (letrozole) and three (anastrozole) disease-gene hits, so the intersection also depends on SwissTargetPrediction."))
S.append(("h2", "3.3 No core-gene expression difference and a VPS35 candidate"))
S.append(("p", "We next asked whether these genes differ between high- and low-BMD bone. GSE35958 re-analysis identified 1,447 DEGs (Fig. 3A), but no core gene was differentially expressed (CYP19A1 borderline, adjusted p = 0.0870; Fig. 3B-C). The independent GSE13850 cohort likewise showed no core-gene differential expression (28 DEGs in total; Fig. 3D), with cross-cohort fold changes in Fig. 3E and DEG totals across the four cohorts in Fig. 3F. In GSE56815 (n = 80), WGCNA gave 13 modules; M4 (r = 0.363) and M5 (r = 0.380) were both higher in low-BMD samples (BH-adjusted p = 0.0066 for each). Both are large (1,743 and 931 genes; median |kME| 0.51 and 0.50), so the association may reflect co-regulated programmes rather than one pathway. LASSO on the M4/M5 kME pool retained VPS35 (Fig. 4A-F; module assignment and module-trait statistics in Table S4 and Table S5)."))
S.append(("p", "Eleven genes entered the model at the minimum-deviance penalty (λ.min = 0.060), and VPS35 also survived the stricter one-standard-error rule (λ.1se = 0.146), where four genes remained (C9orf116, XDH, ADAM11, VPS35). VPS35 was selected in 87.5% of 200 bootstrap resamples, more often than any other gene, and the cross-validated AUC was 0.79 (Fig. 5A-B). The Wnt receptor FZD6 was the transcript most correlated with VPS35 in the nine-sample MSC cohort (r = 0.95, p = 1.1×10-4) but not in the larger monocyte or B-cell cohorts (r = -0.01 to 0.31; Fig. 5C). VPS35 was expressed across independent bone and peripheral-blood datasets (Fig. 5D), including 16 osteoporotic donor MSCs (GSE201774), where it was the most highly expressed of the ten candidate genes (median FPKM 12.97; Fig. 5E; Table S6). Its co-expression module is shown in Fig. 5F. Given the cohort sizes, VPS35 is best regarded as a candidate; the absence of core-gene expression differences fits an exposure-driven hormonal mechanism (RANKL/OPG) rather than a steady-state expression programme {{boyce2007}}. This co-expression pattern fits retromer biology in endosomal sorting {{retromer2023}} and trafficking roles in cancer {{vps35_2021}}, as well as Wnt-modulated cortical bone biology {{sfrp4_2022}}. Osteoporosis multi-omics studies likewise highlight post-transcriptional regulators {{m6a_2024}}. The two monocyte cohorts (GSE56814, n = 73; GSE56815, n = 80) gave 0 and 391 DEGs respectively, none involving a core gene (Fig. S7-S8), and are reported for completeness only."))
S.append(("h2", "3.4 Docking and virtual knockout"))
S.append(("p", "We then asked whether the two leading proteins are engaged directly. Blind docking reproduced the CYP19A1 binding site (-7.22/-7.12 kcal/mol; Fig. 6A) and predicted weaker VPS35 surface binding (-6.03/-6.00 kcal/mol; Fig. S9-S10), below the -7.0 kcal/mol threshold applied to the primary target; scores are listed in Table S7. Virtual knockout of VPS35 in pooled bone-marrow single cells perturbed 25 genes in translation and cell-fusion programmes (Fig. 6B; Table S8), a network-perturbation simulation. The scheme in Fig. 6C uses arrows for hypothetical steps; perturbed programmes were enriched for the terms in Fig. 6D. Poses are summarised in Fig. 6E and Table S7, and the contact residues in Fig. 6F show partly distinct surface patches for the two AIs, which suggests weaker, less specific engagement. Vinardo rescoring agreed: the AIs differed by 0.28 kcal/mol at CYP19A1 (anastrozole marginally better), both scored 0.7-1.2 kcal/mol better than at VPS35, and the drug ranking inverted relative to Vina (Table S9). Top-100 ranking is Table S8 (Fig. S11); FAERS statistics in Table S2, differential expression in Table S10, GSE56815 DEGs in Table S11. No severe or moderate steric clashes occurred (minimum distance 3.02-3.40 Å), and buried areas were 372-391 Å² at aromatase versus 268-311 Å² at VPS35 (Table S12), pointing to shallower engagement of the second target."))

S.append(("h2", "3.5 Cross-species conservation of the CYP19A1 interaction"))
S.append(("p", "To test whether the interaction could operate in non-target vertebrates, we docked both AIs into the zebrafish Cyp19a1a model (AF-O42145-F1). The human and zebrafish enzymes share 51.3% sequence identity overall but are much closer in the binding pocket. Superposing the zebrafish model on the human crystal structure gave 0.60 Å RMSD over 2,537 atoms and 0.29 Å over the nine pocket Cα atoms. Re-docking the co-crystallised ligand recovered its pose at 0.32 Å RMSD, confirming that the protocol reproduces a known binding mode. Nine of the ten human active-site residues in contact with the ligand have identical counterparts in zebrafish (ARG115→ARG135, ILE132/ILE133→ILE152/ILE153, PHE221→PHE241, TRP224→W244, VAL370→VAL389, PHE430→PHE449, LEU477→LEU496, SER478→SER497), and the single substitution (PHE148→TYR168) is conservative. Both inhibitors docked at least as favourably into the fish orthologue (letrozole -7.63 versus -7.22 kcal/mol in human CYP19A1; anastrozole -7.20 versus -7.12; Fig. 7A-C; Table S13 and Table S14). These docking estimates are compatible with fish exposure engaging the same initiating event, but they do not show that environmental concentrations reach the active site."))

S.append(("h2", "3.6 Summary of the evidence"))
S.append(("p", "The five analyses converge on CYP19A1 but differ in strength. Pharmacovigilance provides the strongest evidence (E-values above 4.9). CYP19A1 sits at the intersection of the drug-target and disease-gene sets, whereas the PPI layer itself is organised around the ADRB/HTR cluster. Both transcriptomic cohorts are negative for the core genes, which limits the claim to exposure-associated risk, and the docking and knockout results are predictions only."))
S.append(("h1", "4. Discussion"))
S.append(("p", "This study links the clinical bone safety of aromatase inhibitors to the molecular pharmacology of their target. FAERS gives consistent signals, the target intersection is anchored on CYP19A1, and computational layers nominate VPS35. The translational implication, bone-protective co-management of AI therapy, rests primarily on the pharmacovigilance layer, the strongest evidence presented, and is consistent with current guideline-based management of cancer treatment-induced bone loss {{hadji2025, coleman2020}}."))
S.append(("p", "The proposed axis is biologically plausible. Aromatase inhibition lowers oestradiol, releasing the brake on RANKL-driven osteoclastogenesis {{boyce2007}}, which is consistent with an exposure-driven rather than dose-dependent signal. CYP19A1 is the shared target, and the adrenergic and serotonergic components of the network point to established regulators of bone mass {{takeda2002, yadav2008}}. VPS35 is the cargo-recognition subunit of the retromer trimer {{seaman2012}}; retromer-dependent recycling controls surface availability of Wnt receptors such as FZD6, and loss of this sorting phenocopies reduced Wnt responsiveness {{retromer2023, seaman2012}}. Wnt signalling is a central regulator of bone formation {{baron2013}}; the correlation of VPS35 with FZD6 in the MSC cohort, together with the role of SFRP4 in cortical bone {{sfrp4_2022}}, makes the candidate testable."))
S.append(("p", "Across two independent cohorts (bone-marrow MSCs, n = 9; circulating B lymphocytes, n = 20), none of the nine core genes differed significantly between high- and low-BMD donors. The consistency across cohorts suggests that the AI-bone axis does not manifest as steady-state expression differences in these small datasets. The pharmacovigilance signal is therefore better read as exposure-associated risk than as an expression signature."))
S.append(("p", "Several limitations apply. Spontaneous reporting carries underreporting, notoriety and channelling biases that the tamoxifen control quantifies but does not remove; the crossed drug-event ranking may reflect prescribing patterns and not pharmacology, and exemestane-fracture was uninformative. The transcriptomic layers are underpowered (n = 9 and n = 20), and GSE56815 carries no age, BMI or bone-density covariates, so module-trait associations are unadjusted. WGCNA and LASSO results are therefore exploratory, with quality checks in Table S15. Blind docking without molecular dynamics is exploratory; the near-identical CYP19A1 scores do not reproduce the experimental ranking. Virtual knockout lacks cell-type resolution."))
S.append(("p", "Environmental implications follow from the same target. These drugs enter wastewater; anastrozole has been quantified in aqueous matrices and treated by advanced oxidation {{anastrozole_water}}, and tamoxifen reached 5-19 ng/g dry weight in sludge while letrozole was mostly below quantification {{ai_env_sludge}}. Table S16 compiles measured concentrations; the highest sludge value (tamoxifen, 19 ng/g dry weight) lies far below the ng/L to µg/L range producing effects in fish {{ees_letrozole_tilapia, letrozole_zebrafish, medaka_antiestrogen}}, and wild-fish exposure remains unquantified. To move beyond a plausibility argument, we applied the fish plasma model used in pharmaceutical read-across {{randweaver2013, margiotta2014}} to the three drugs. Plasma partitioning followed log K(plasma) = 0.73 logKow − 0.16, with logKow from PubChem {{pubchem2025}} and human therapeutic concentrations from prescribing information {{labels_ai}}. Across a 1-100 ng/L water scenario, the exposure-activity ratio (EAR) rises from 0.001-0.007 to 0.09-0.70, i.e. exemestane approaches therapeutic plasma levels at 100 ng/L while letrozole and anastrozole remain 4- and 8-fold below (Fig. 7D; Table S17). Because systematic measured concentrations are lacking for these compounds, this is a scenario bracket, not a site-specific risk estimate. Cross-species docking (-7.63/-7.20 kcal/mol for zebrafish Cyp19a1a versus -7.22/-7.12 for human, with 9/10 active-site residues identical; Fig. 7) shows residue-level conservation. Read as an adverse outcome pathway, CYP19A1 inhibition is the initiating event, altered steroidogenesis the key event, and bone or gonadal phenotypes the outcomes (Fig. 7E). This AOP is a hypothesis and is not registered in AOPWiki. The adrenergic and serotonergic nodes suggest key events extend beyond gonadal histology to skeletogenic and neuroendocrine readouts. Environmental risk assessment of these drugs should therefore include steroidogenic and skeletogenic endpoints, and the VPS35/Wnt arm offers a candidate for cross-species read-across {{seaman2012, baron2013}}. No environmental exposure was measured here, so the comparison with fish remains indirect."))

S.append(("p", "Future work should include FAERS trend analyses with exposure proxies, molecular-dynamics refinement of the VPS35 poses, functional validation in osteoblasts and prospective epidemiology. The crossed ranking parallels inconclusive head-to-head comparisons, and channeling could produce it, so the two drugs are not ranked. Exemestane shows a class-consistent osteoporosis signal (ROR 3.61) but uninformative fracture data, leaving the steroidal-versus-nonsteroidal question open. Pending validation, VPS35 remains a candidate, not a biomarker."))
S.append(("h1", "5. Conclusions"))
S.append(("p", "Network toxicology integration identifies an axis that links aromatase-inhibitor exposure and CYP19A1 inhibition to oestrogen depletion, VPS35-centred network perturbation and osteoporosis. Pharmacovigilance provides the strongest evidence, both transcriptomic cohorts are consistently negative, and VPS35 together with the docking predictions remains a hypothesis awaiting molecular-dynamics refinement and experimental validation."))

